L000118	\begin{abstract}
L000119	    Quantum devices featuring mid-circuit measurement and reset capabilities, such as quantum computers and dual-species Rydberg quantum simulators, enable the realization of {\it quantum cellular automata}.
L000120	
L000121	    These systems evolve in discrete time following local updates implemented by unitary gates, and allow for the realization of both closed and synthetic dissipative dynamics.
L000122	
L000123	    Here, we focus on quantum cellular automata that implement minimal models of classical and quantum transport.
L000124	
L000125	    To illustrate our ideas, we focus on a discrete-time totally asymmetric simple exclusion process and investigate how coherent dynamical contributions allow for the emergence of quantum effects and correlations.
L000126	
L000127	    We find that bipartite entanglement dominates the transient evolution, while stationary states can retain quantum correlations beyond entanglement.
L000128	
L000129	    Our results suggest viable routes for realizing transport models on quantum devices and characterizing collective quantum correlations in strongly driven systems.
L000130	\end{abstract}
L000131	
L000132	
L000133	
L000134	\maketitle
L000135	
L000136	
L000137	{\bf Introduction.---}Transport phenomena provide a unifying framework for the study of nonequilibrium systems.
L000138	
L000139	At the microscopic scale, particle transport is often modeled using {\it simple exclusion processes}, which describe the stochastic dynamics of mobile agents on lattices and complex networks.
L000140	
L000141	These processes have found a wide range of applications including RNA polymerization~\cite{MacDonald68,Lakatos2003,Chowdhury08,Scott2019,Juraj23}, transport through networks~\cite{Neri11,Bun14}, and traffic modeling~\cite{Nagel96,Bonnin2022}.
L000142	
L000143	Among the many variants, the asymmetric simple exclusion process (ASEP) and its totally asymmetric counterpart (TASEP) have emerged as  paradigmatic nonequilibrium models, characterized by a rich phenomenology, multiple stationary phases, with the stationary state exactly solvable in the classical case~\cite{Prol16,Ada07,Gor12,Gier11,Dep04,Brank00,Bun24,Blythe07,Derrida_1993,Derrida98,Schtz1997,Golinelli2006,Rkos2005,Spohn2006,Raj97,Chou2011,Hel01,Kavanagh_2022,Temme_2012,Blythe07}.
L000144	
L000145	
L000146	
L000147	
L000148	\begin{figure}[t]
L000149	    \centering
L000150	    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{Fig1.png}
L000151	    \caption{ {\bf Quantum cellular automaton from dual-species atom arrays.}  (a) Two-species atom array (sketched on the left) modeled of system qubits (left most in red) and ancillary qubits (right most in green).
L000152	
L000153	    System and ancillary qubits interact through the repeated application of the local quantum gate $G$.
L000154	
L000155	    After a full sweep throughout the lattice, the ancilla qubits are measured and reinitialized and the sweep is repeated.
L000156	
L000157	    (b) The gate $G$ is composed of two unitary gates: $U$ encodes coherent interactions within the system, $D$ induces ancilla-mitigated dissipation in the system.
L000158	
L000159	    SWAP operations are inserted to stress the nearest-neighbor range of the gate $U$ and to ensure the final qubit order is consistent with the initial qubit order; they do not affect the effective transport dynamics.
L000160	
L000161	    (c) The full QCA update, consisting of sweep and ancilla reinitialization, implements a dissipative dynamics that can be expressed as a global Kraus map ${\cal K}$ that propagates the quantum state through time $\rho_{t+1} = {\cal K} [\rho_t]$.
L000162	    }
L000163	    \label{fig:sketch}
L000164	\end{figure}
L000165	
L000166	
L000167	
L000168	
L000169	{\it Cellular automata} (CA) are minimal discrete-time models whose fundamental units, so called cells, are updated according to local rules depending on the state of the neighboring cells~\cite{Wolf83,Mart84}.
L000170	
L000171	Famous examples of CA include Conway's Game of Life~\cite{Gard70} or Wolfram's Rule 30 automaton~\cite{Wolf83,Wolf85}, both of which are of particular interest as they produce complex behavior from seemingly simplistic rules.
L000172	
L000173	Their ability to generate emergent complexity motivates the study of CA dynamics and makes them a versatile tool for designing synthetic dynamical systems.
L000174	
L000175	Due to their locality, CA provide a natural minimal framework for modeling simple exclusion processes.
L000176	
L000177	
L000178	CA have also been extended to the quantum setting.
L000179	
L000180	Here, each cell consists of a qubit (or more generally a $d$-level quantum system, known as a qudit), and the update rules are replaced by local unitary gates~\cite{Farrelly2020}, see Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}(a).
L000181	
L000182	Quantum CA (QCA) have recently been realized experimentally in programmable atomic arrays~\cite{White2026}.
L000183	
L000184	Mid-circuit measurements and qubit resets enable the implementation of incoherent stochastic processes, allowing to engineer synthetic open-system dynamics.
L000185	
L000186	In these settings, QCA have been identified as a potential approach to overcoming challenges associated with reliable manipulation of large many-qubit systems as their simple structure can limit the complexity of the coherent dynamical control~\cite{Cesa2026,White2026}.
L000187	
L000188	Furthermore, QCA have been utilized, both theoretically and experimentally, to explore nonequilibrium dynamics~\cite{Duranthon21,Gill20,sfairopoulos25} as well as phase transitions into absorbing states~\cite{Lesa19} and collective properties in quantum neural networks~\cite{Wintermantel20,Gill23,Boneberg_24,Boneberg_25}.
L000189	
L000190	However, QCA have not been used for exploring quantum transport, which is the main purpose of this paper, particularly since discrete-time dynamics can lead to qualitatively different correlation structures~\cite{Wald2025}.
L000191	
L000192	
L000193	Recently, quantum versions of simple exclusion processes have attracted significant interest~\cite{Albert2026, Bernard21, Bernard22, Bernard25, Alba2025,Russotto2025}.
L000194	
L000195	Notably, the TASEP has emerged as a fundamental building block of {\it active quantum matter}~\cite{Kha24}.
L000196	
L000197	Despite this progress, the role of genuine quantum correlations in driven exclusion dynamics remains poorly understood, particularly in the nonequilibrium steady states (NESS) arising from the interplay between coherent and stochastic transport.
L000198	
L000199	
L000200	In this work, we introduce a QCA realization of the TASEP and explore the competition of classical stochastic and coherent quantum transport.
L000201	
L000202	Recent experiments in programmable Rydberg atom arrays have demonstrated scalable control of many-body quantum dynamics, placing the systems considered here within experimental reach~\cite{Labuhn2016, Bernien2017, Brow2020, Ebadi2021}.
L000203	
L000204	We investigate the nonequilibrium phase diagram of this QCA-implementation of the quantum TASEP through large-scale simulations, and characterize the resulting NESS.
L000205	
L000206	Strikingly, although standard entanglement witnesses do not detect quantum correlations, the steady states exhibit clear quantum signatures beyond entanglement.
L000207	
L000208	Given the simplicity and paradigmatic nature of the TASEP, our results indicate that quantum features beyond entanglement can appear even in minimal models of dissipative transport.\\
L000209	
L000210	
L000211	\begin{figure*}[t]
L000212	    \centering
L000213	    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig2.png}
L000214	    \caption{{\bf Average lattice occupation in finite-size NESS.} (a) Phase diagram for a system of $N=30$ lattice sites with coherent transport induced by the gate $U(\pi/4)$ and bulk hopping probability $\tau =0.75$.
L000215	
L000216	    The dashed line indicates the classical coexistence line separating the LD and HD phases and the solid line indicates the position of the transition to the MC phase in the classical model ($\omega =0$).
L000217	
L000218	    Colored markers indicate parameter values we further study in Fig.~\ref{fig:LQU_and_Neg} and colored lines indicate parameter ranges for the order parameter scalings in panels (b)-(d).
L000219	
L000220	    Panel (b) shows the finite-size scaling of the average lattice occupation across the coexistence line ($\beta = 0.3$) and panels (c) and (d) show the finite-size scaling of the derivative of the average lattice occupation across the LD-MC ($\beta = 0.7$) and the HD-MC transition ($\alpha = 0.7$) respectively.
L000221	
L000222	    All panels depict lattice sizes $N = 6,8,10,12,14,16,18,20,22,26,30$ encoded by the opacity of the lines with larger $N$ being more opaque.
L000223	    }
L000224	    \label{fig:PDq}
L000225	\end{figure*}
L000226	
L000227	
L000228	
L000229	
L000230	{\bf Transport QCA model.---}We consider the quantum circuit model depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}(a).
L000231	
L000232	Each lattice site hosts a qubit, whose $Z$ eigenstates are interpreted as occupied $\ket{\bullet}$ or vacant $\ket{\circ}$, representing agents in a one-dimensional lattice.
L000233	
L000234	Two neighboring qubits are paired with one ancillary qubit to form a {\it cluster}.
L000235	
L000236	The automaton rule, realized by the quantum gate $G$, is applied on these clusters.
L000237	
L000238	We explicitly consider a gate that consists of the subsequent application of a two-qubit gate $U$ followed by a three-qubit gate $D$, see Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}(b).
L000239	
L000240	The gate $U$ acts solely on the system qubits, whereas $D$ allows the ancilla to interact with the system qubits.
L000241	
L000242	As depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}(a), all ancilla qubits are measured and reinitialized once the automaton rule has been applied throughout the lattice, thereby inducing dissipative processes in the system.
L000243	
L000244	For the clusters we introduce the eight quantum states $\kett{\threestate{\halfcirc}{\halfcirc}{\halfcirc}}$ where the ancilla label is chosen to sit on top of the two system labels, respecting the triangular structure introduced in Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}.
L000245	
L000246	Upon measuring the ancilla, the QCA rule implements a dissipative quantum channel in each system cluster which is described by two Kraus operators $K_{\halfcirc} = \brat{\threestate{\halfcirc}{\ }{\ }} D \kett{\threestate{\circ}{\ }{\ }}$~\cite{SM} corresponding to the measurement outcome.
L000247	
L000248	This setup allows us to include a single dissipative processes per cluster in the otherwise coherent QCA.
L000249	
L000250	An ancilla flip indicates if the corresponding dissipative process has occurred in a measured realization.
L000251	
L000252	This scheme, can also be thought of as a repeated interaction framework~\cite{Karevski09}, in which successive layers of ancillary qubits interact with a system through the repeated application of the QCA rule, implemented by a quantum gate~\cite{Gill20,Gill21,Gill22,Gill23,Boneberg_2023}.
L000253	
L000254	To explore quantum transport phenomena in this QCA framework, we choose the automaton rule $G$ as follows.
L000255	
L000256	The two-qubit system gate is the parameterized gate~\footnote{As $U$ does not act on the ancilla, we discard its label here.}
L000257	
L000258	
L000259	\begin{align}
L000260	\begin{split}
L000261	U(\omega)
L000262	&=\ket{\circ\circ}\bra{\circ\circ}+ (\cos\omega \ket{\circ\bullet} - i\sin\omega\ket{\bullet\circ})\bra{\circ\bullet}\\
L000263	&+(\cos\omega \ket{\bullet\circ} - i\sin\omega \ket{\circ\bullet})\bra{\bullet\circ} + \ket{\bullet\bullet}\bra{\bullet\bullet}
L000264	\end{split}
L000265	\end{align}
L000266	
L000267	
L000268	which implements coherent nearest-neighbor hopping of excitations.
L000269	
L000270	
L000271	The system-ancilla component is
L000272	\begin{align}
L000273	\begin{split}
L000274	D(\tau)
L000275	=\mathds{1} &+ i\sqrt{\tau}(\kett{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}\brat{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}
L000276	+\kett{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}\brat{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}})\\
L000277	&- (1-\sqrt{1-\tau})(\kett{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}\brat{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}+ \kett{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}\brat{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}})
L000278	\end{split}
L000279	\end{align}
L000280	
L000281	
L000282	which implements the stochastic TASEP update rule upon tracing over the ancilla~\cite{SM}.
L000283	
L000284	Since the ancilla is in the vacant state at the start of each time update, we see that, apart from the identity, only two terms in $D(\tau)$ contribute.
L000285	
L000286	These correspond to a hopping transition term if the ancilla flips with probability $\tau$ ($\kett{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}\brat{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}$) and a no-hop term if the ancilla does not flip
L000287	($\kett{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}\brat{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}$).
L000288	
L000289	Hence, in the absence of the gate $U(\omega)$, the repeated ancilla interaction implements the bulk rules of a discrete-time TASEP with the bulk hopping probability $\tau$.
L000290	
L000291	To realize open-boundary TASEP dynamics, we consider a boundary driven system~\cite{Prosen11,Karevski13,Caro18,Prosen11,Zni14,Zni14b,Ilievski2014}.
L000292	
L000293	At the left boundary, excitations are injected with probability $\alpha$, while at the right boundary they are ejected with probability $\beta$.
L000294	
L000295	These boundary processes can be implemented using two additional ancillary qubits that interact locally with the left and right boundary qubit respectively, in direct analogy to the ancilla-mediated bulk hopping, see the Supplemental Material for further details~\cite{SM}.
L000296	
L000297	
L000298	The stationary state for the classical TASEP can be analytically derived using a matrix product ansatz~\cite{Brank00,SM} and supports three distinct non-equilibrium phases.
L000299	
L000300	A high density phase (HD) is separated from a low density phase (LD) by the coexistence line $\alpha = \beta$ up to $\alpha^\star = \beta^\star=1-\sqrt{1-\tau}$.
L000301	
L000302	For $\alpha, \beta > \alpha^\star, \beta^\star$ the TASEP supports  a maximum current phase (MC).
L000303	
L000304	While both the LD-MC and the HD-MC transition are continuous, the LD-HD transition is discontinuous~\cite{Brank00,SM}.
L000305	
L000306	For the remainder of this work we choose $\tau = 0.75$.\\
L000307	
L000308	
L000309	{\bf Quantum NESS phases.---}We explore the influence of coherent transport induced by $U(\omega)$ on the phase diagram of the classical TASEP.
L000310	
L000311	We note that $U(\omega)$ is the identity in the zero- and the two-particle sector of each cluster and acts non-trivially only in the single-particle sector.
L000312	
L000313	For $\omega = \pi/4$ this gate can create maximally entangled Bell states between neighboring system sites, such that the influence of the coherent transport is expected to be most significant.
L000314	
L000315	Thus we restrict our analysis to $\omega=\pi/4$ for the remainder of this work.
L000316	
L000317	
L000318	We simulate the QCA model using a time-evolving block decimation (TEBD) tensor-network algorithm~\cite{Vidal04,Vidal07}, allowing the bond dimension to adapt dynamically during the evolution.
L000319	
L000320	A single time step consists of the iterative application of the left boundary drive, the sequential bulk update,
L000321	and the right boundary drive, see Supplemental Material for additional details~\cite{SM}.
L000322	
L000323	This procedure allows us to time-evolve the system to its NESS determined by the boundary driving parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$.
L000324	
L000325	Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq} shows our findings for systems up to $N=30$ qubits.
L000326	
L000327	
L000328	We see from Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(a) that the general shape of the classical phase diagram stays intact upon the introduction of strong coherent transport.
L000329	
L000330	In Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(b)-(d) we illustrate the relevant order parameter crossings of the average lattice density that define the exact location of the phase boundaries.
L000331	
L000332	Upon increasing the system size $N$ across the LD-HD coexistence line, the  average occupation decreases for smaller $\alpha$ and increases for larger $\alpha$.
L000333	
L000334	This behavior is indicated by a crossing of the finite-size curves and suggests the emergence of a jump discontinuity, see Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(b).
L000335	
L000336	Conversely, the LD-MC as well as the HD-MC transition remain continuous and only the derivative of the average number density develops a discontinuity.
L000337	
L000338	However, the extent of the MC phase is diminished compared to the classical case as can be seen from the finite-size scaling analysis in Figs.~\ref{fig:PDq}(c) and~(d).
L000339	
L000340	As we explained above, the onset of the MC phase in the classical case depends on the numerical value of the bulk hopping probability $\tau$.
L000341	
L000342	Larger $\tau$ values diminish the size of the MC phase, similar to what we observe in the presence of coherent transport.
L000343	
L000344	Thus, the coherent transport appears to renormalize the bulk hopping probability towards higher values, which we further analyse in the Supplemental Material~\cite{SM}.
L000345	
L000346	The collective behavior of the quantum model seems therefore largely governed by the underlying classical transport.
L000347	
L000348	Remarkably, we will show below that the NESS still exhibits clear quantum correlations.\\
L000349	
L000350	
L000351	{\bf Quantum correlations.---}We consider the build-up of quantum correlations during the time evolution.
L000352	
L000353	The half-system negativity is defined as ${\cal N}= (||\rho^\Gamma||_1-1)/2 $, where $\rho^\Gamma$ denotes the partially transposed density matrix.
L000354	
L000355	${\cal N}$ can witness entanglement via detection of violations of the positivity of the partial transpose $\rho^\Gamma$ (PPT criterion)~\cite{Vidal02,Plenio05}.
L000356	
L000357	From Fig.~\ref{fig:LQU_and_Neg}(a)-(c) we generally observe that the negativity initially increases sharply in all regions of the phase diagram.
L000358	
L000359	
L000360	\begin{figure*}[t]
L000361	\centering
L000362	    \includegraphics[width= .9\textwidth]{Fig3_column.png}
L000363	    \caption{\textbf{Quantum correlations}.   (a)-(c) Time evolution of the half-system entanglement negativity for $N=6,8,10,12,14$ in the MC, HD and LD phase respectively (see markers in Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(a)).
L000364	
L000365	    Panels (d) and (e) show the maximum two-site LQU and coherence with respect to the central bulk qubit across the HD-LD transition ($\beta=0.3$).
L000366	
L000367	    Panels (f) and (g) show the derivative of the same quantities across the LD-MC transitions ($\beta =0.7$). All figures from panels (d)-(g) are plotted for $N=6,8,10,12,14,16,18,20,22,26,30.$
L000368	    }
L000369	    \label{fig:LQU_and_Neg}
L000370	\end{figure*}
L000371	
L000372	
L000373	This is expected as initially quantum correlations are generated through coherent transport.
L000374	
L000375	The behavior after the initial peak depends on the region of the phase diagram.
L000376	
L000377	In the MC phase, the negativity vanishes rapidly following this initial spike and increasing system sizes do not support a longer-lived negativity.
L000378	
L000379	Conversely in the LD and HD phase, as the system size increases, the initial negativity spike broadens, indicating that the corresponding entanglement survives for longer times when the system size is increased.
L000380	
L000381	However, in the finite-size settings, the negativity eventually dies out and does not play a role in the correlation structure of the finite-size NESS.
L000382	
L000383	We have also verified that all two-site reduced density matrix in the NESS are separable and that moment ratios of the partially transposed density matrix~\cite{Cara24} of the NESS for larger system sizes do not violate the PPT criterion~\cite{SM}, further substantiating the claim that generally the finite-size NESS correlations are not dominated by bipartite entanglement.
L000384	
L000385	Our findings in the HD and LD phases indicate that a finite entanglement negativity may persist in the thermodynamic limit, because the time over which it survives increases with system size.
L000386	
L000387	This contrasts with prior observations of weak correlation signatures in strongly driven systems~\cite{Temme_2012}, and reveals that substantial quantum correlations may persist in the steady state.
L000388	
L000389	
L000390	Since we find that the negativity consistently vanishes in the long-time limit in our simulations, it raises the question whether other types of quantum correlations may be present in the NESS.
L000391	
L000392	Thus, we consider the local quantum uncertainty (LQU) ${\cal U}$~\cite{Giro13} which is closely related to quantum discord (see~\cite{Bera18} for a review) and a coherence measure $C$ in the classical basis that quantifies superpositions.
L000393	
L000394	To evaluate these quantities, we first pick a central bulk site at site $c=N/2$ and compute all two-site reduced density matrices $\rho_{j,c}$.
L000395	
L000396	We then evaluate ${\cal U}$ by performing local measurements on the central qubit and $C$ by summing the absolute values of all off-diagonal elements of  $\rho_{j,c}$ and consider their maximum across the phase boundaries.
L000397	
L000398	These measures are particularly appealing because they are experimentally accessible via two-site quantum state tomography~\cite{QSE,Auc11,Kim23,Ram21}.
L000399	
L000400	
L000401	The results are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:LQU_and_Neg}(d)-(g).
L000402	
L000403	Across the LD-HD transition we see that interestingly both $\mathcal{U}$ and $C$ clearly witness the transition by an uptake in absolute value around the transition point, see Figs.~\ref{fig:LQU_and_Neg}(d) and~(e).
L000404	
L000405	However, both phases do show extensive $\mathcal{U}$ for the system sizes we investigated whereas $C$ indicates a crossing in the vicinity of the coexistence line.
L000406	
L000407	Across the continuous LD-MC transition, $\partial_\alpha C$ shows a crossing in the vicinity of the critical point whereas the derivative $\partial_{\alpha}\mathcal{U}$ shows a strong drop upon entering the MC phase.
L000408	
L000409	We observed similar results across the remaining HD-MC transition.
L000410	
L000411	These results indicate that local two-site superpositions in the bulk are most pronounced in the MC phase and more pronounced in the HD phase than the LD phase.
L000412	
L000413	This can be understood from the structure of the mixed state, whose dominant weight comes from pure states with nearest-neighbor empty–full pairings ($\kett{\circ\bullet},\kett{\bullet\circ}$), implying that local clusters are typically in the single-particle sector.
L000414	
L000415	These states are mapped by the coherent transport $U$ into local superpositions whereas the zero- and two-particle sectors are not mapped into superpositions.
L000416	
L000417	Therefore, the HD phase which has larger overlap with the single-particle sector than the LD phase also shows larger superpositions.
L000418	
L000419	This explains the steep signal of the coherence and the LQU  across the LD-HD transition since the typical two-site configurations change drastically across the coexistence line.
L000420	
L000421	Moreover, the MC phase is a homogeneous phase with the largest contribution from the single-particle sector and thus the most superpositions.
L000422	
L000423	This indicates that our quantum witnesses do flatten out in the MC phase, yielding a diminished derivative as can be seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:LQU_and_Neg}.
L000424	
L000425	
L000426	Thus, the LQU and the coherence qualitatively reproduce the phase diagram from Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}.
L000427	
L000428	In Fig.~\ref{fig:coh_lqu} we show the results for ${\cal U}$ and $C$ for $N=30$ obtained from the matrix product states that also produced the occupation phase diagram in Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(a).
L000429	
L000430	
L000431	\begin{figure}[t]
L000432	\centering
L000433	    \includegraphics[width= \columnwidth]{Fig4.png}
L000434	    \caption{ {\bf Phase diagram from quantum correlations.} Panels (a) and (b) show  the two-site the coherence and LQU respectively, as described in the text, for the NESS that produced Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(a).}
L000435	    \label{fig:coh_lqu}
L000436	\end{figure}
L000437	
L000438	
L000439	We see that both witnesses reproduce the structure of the quantum phase diagram, indicating that quantum correlations beyond entanglement are an essential feature of the NESS and are capable of detecting non-equilibrium phase transitions.\\
L000440	
L000441	
L000442	{\bf Conclusion.---}We introduced a QCA model for coherent and stochastic transport and analyzed a quantum extension of the paradigmatic TASEP.
L000443	
L000444	While the quantum dynamics largely preserves the classical transport behavior at the level of local observables, leaving the phase diagram essentially unchanged, its correlation structure is fundamentally different.
L000445	
L000446	We established that entanglement plays a crucial role in the approach to the NESS, with its decay sharply distinguishing between the different phases.
L000447	
L000448	Strikingly, although no bipartite entanglement is detected in the NESS, the steady state exhibits clear quantum correlations beyond entanglement, including discord and coherence.
L000449	
L000450	It would be interesting to further explore how these correlations affect the broader thermodynamic properties of the NESS~\cite{Stra17} and their impact on transport in realistic complex networks.
L000451	
L000452	Our results show that nonclassical correlations persist in dissipative transport systems and even encode collective properties such as the phase structure in the absence of entanglement.
L000453	
L000454	This establishes dissipative quantum transport as a setting where all quantum correlations remain relevant.
L000455	
L000456	More broadly, our results suggest that strongly driven quantum systems generically host rich quantum correlation structures.\\
