L000527	\section{Kraus map}
L000528	In this section we derive the Kraus map that governs the QCA update rule.
L000529	
L000530	We focus first on a single three-qubit cluster and then build the global map from this elementary building block.
L000531	
L000532	Since the Kraus map for a cluster is obtained by tracing out the ancilla, we omit the system gate $U$ here for clarity.
L000533	
L000534	For completeness, we recall the form of the three-qubit gate $D$:
L000535	
L000536	
L000537	\begin{align}
L000538	D=\mathds{1} + i\sqrt{\tau}(\kett{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}\brat{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}
L000539	+\kett{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}\brat{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}})
L000540	- (1-\sqrt{1-\tau})(\kett{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}\brat{\threestate{\bullet}{\circ}{\bullet}}+ \kett{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}\brat{\threestate{\circ}{\bullet}{\circ}}) \ .
L000541	\end{align}
L000542	
L000543	
L000544	The Kraus operators are simply obtained by considering that the repeated interaction implies that the ancilla is always initialized in the vacant state $\ket{\circ}$.
L000545	
L000546	However, after the system-ancilla interaction, the ancilla may be found either in the vacant state $\ket{\circ}$ or the occupied state $\ket{\bullet}$.
L000547	
L000548	These two outcomes give rise to two Kraus operators $K_\circ$ and $K_\bullet$, labeled by the post-interaction ancilla state.
L000549	
L000550	Hence, we find the cluster Kraus operators induced by the QCA update, viz.
L000551	
L000552	
L000553	\begin{align}
L000554	    K_\circ &= \brat{\threestate{\circ}{\ }{\ }} D \kett{\threestate{\circ}{\ }{\ }} =
L000555	    \mathds{1} - (1-\sqrt{1-\tau})  \kett{\bullet\circ}\brat{\bullet\circ},\\
L000556	
L000557	    K_\bullet &= \brat{\threestate{\bullet}{\ }{\ }} D \kett{\threestate{\circ}{\ }{\ }} =
L000558	     i\sqrt{\tau} \kett{\circ\bullet}\brat{\bullet\circ} \ .
L000559	\end{align}
L000560	
L000561	
L000562	It is easy to verify that these operators form a valid Kraus map $K_\circ^\dagger K_\circ +  K_\bullet^\dagger K_\bullet = \mathds{1}$.
L000563	
L000564	The inclusion of the unitary gate $U$ simply dresses these Kraus operators with a $U$ from the right.
L000565	
L000566	The global update of the system density matrix can thus be written as
L000567	
L000568	\begin{align} \label{evol}
L000569	    \rho(t+1) = \mathcal{K}_{R} \circ \mathcal{K}_{N-1} \circ \cdots \mathcal{K}_1 \circ \mathcal{K}_L [\rho(t)],
L000570	\end{align}
L000571	
L000572	
L000573	with $\mathcal{K}_j[ \cdot  ] = K^{(j)}_\circ  \cdot  K^{(j)\dagger}_\circ +  K^{(j)}_\bullet \cdot K^{(j)\dagger}_\bullet$ and $j$ indicating the bond $(j,j+1)$.
L000574	
L000575	$\mathcal{K}_{R/L}$ being the right/left most boundary drive Kraus maps respectively.
L000576	
L000577	These can be derived in a similar fashion considering the boundary sites interacting with an additional ancilla through the following gates respectively.
L000578	
L000579	
L000580	\begin{align}
L000581	    D_L &=\mathds{1} +i \sqrt{\alpha}(\kett{\twostate{\bullet}{\bullet}} \brat{\twostate{\circ}{\circ}}+\kett{\twostate{\circ}{\circ}} \brat{\twostate{\bullet}{\bullet}})-(1-\sqrt{
L000582	    1-\alpha})(\kett{\twostate{\circ}{\circ}} \brat{\twostate{\circ}{\circ}}+\kett{\twostate{\bullet}{\bullet}} \brat{\twostate{\bullet}{\bullet}}),\\
L000583	    D_R &= \mathds{1}+i \sqrt{\beta} (\kett{\twostate{\bullet}{\circ}} \brat{\twostate{\circ}{\bullet}}+\kett{\twostate{\circ}{\bullet}} \brat{\twostate{\bullet}{\circ}}) -(1- \sqrt{1-\beta}) (\kett{\twostate{\bullet}{\circ}} \brat{\twostate{\bullet}{\circ}}+\kett{\twostate{\circ}{\bullet}} \brat{\twostate{\circ}{\bullet}})\ .
L000584	\end{align}
L000585	
L000586	
L000587	Here, similar to the three-qubit gate, the ancilla label is chosen on top of the system label.
