L000592	\section{Classical TASEP}
L000593	
L000594	In this section we first review the solution of the steady state of the classical TASEP through a matrix product ansatz.
L000595	
L000596	We focus on the discrete-time case with forward sequential update, since this scenario is considered in the main text.
L000597	
L000598	However, the ansatz can be easily adjusted for other update rules or to continuous time.
L000599	
L000600	We then discuss the phase diagram of the TASEP through finite-size scaling obtained from the exact solution.
L000601	
L000602	\subsection{Solution for the forward sequential update}
L000603	
L000604	\begin{figure*}[t]
L000605	\centering
L000606	    \includegraphics[width= 0.5\textwidth]{qca_new.png}
L000607	    \caption{ {\bf Circuit Diagram for the sequential update rule.} The figure shows a circuit diagram for the sequential TASEP update with matrix product ansatz $\rho =\langle W| M^{\otimes N}|V\rangle$.}
L000608	    \label{fig:S1}
L000609	\end{figure*}
L000610	
L000611	
L000612	Here we sketch the derivation of the exact NESS $\rho_{\rm ss}$ of the classical TASEP with the forward sequential update as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:S1}.
L000613	
L000614	To this end, we make the ansatz $\rho_{\rm ss}= \bra{W} M \cdots M\ket{V}$ for a chain with $N$ sites.
L000615	
L000616	Here $M$ is the repeated on-site tensor and $\bra{W}$ and $\ket{V}$ are boundary vectors.
L000617	
L000618	A single time step is then given by the application of $\mathcal{K} = \mathcal{K}_R \circ \mathcal{K}_{N-1} \circ \cdots \circ \mathcal{K}_1 \circ \mathcal{K}_L$, see Fig.~\ref{fig:S1}.
L000619	
L000620	If the NESS satisfies the following conditions
L000621	
L000622	
L000623	\begin{align} \label{DT:telescope}
L000624	    \bra{W}\mathcal{K}_L M = \bra{W}\hat{M}, \qquad
L000625	    \mathcal{K}_j \hat{M}M = M\hat{M},   \qquad
L000626	    \mathcal{K}_R \hat{M}\ket{V} = M\ket{V}
L000627	\end{align}
L000628	
L000629	
L000630	then the state is stationary by construction, i.e. $\mathcal{K} \rho_{\rm ss} = \rho_{\rm ss}$.
L000631	
L000632	Hence, we may use these conditions to determine a self-consistent representation of the NESS.
L000633	
L000634	It is important to note that this approach also technically holds for the quantum case but the resulting equations are significantly more complicated.
L000635	
L000636	However, an analytical solution for the boundary driven case in the absence of bulk drive, i.e. $\tau=0$, does exist~\cite{Prosen11}.
L000637	
L000638	In the classical case ($U_j = \mathds{1})$ we may express the tensor $M$ in terms of the physical dimension as $M = \operatorname{diag}(F, E)$.
L000639	
L000640	Here, $F$ and $E$ are matrices that we will determine and the fact that the system is classical allows us to neglect coherences, i.e. consider $M$ to be diagonal.
L000641	
L000642	From the bulk condition ($0<j<N$) we then find
L000643	
L000644	\begin{align} \label{eq:ana_class_bulk_deri}
L000645	\begin{split}
L000646	    \mathcal{K}_j (\hat{M} M)
L000647	    &=\tau (\sigma^-\hat{M}\sigma^+)   (\sigma^+ M \sigma^-)
L000648	    +  \hat{M} M
L000649	    -(1-\sqrt{1-\tau}) \left[ (\hat{M}n) (M  \bar{n})
L000650	    +(n  \hat{M}) (\bar{n} M)
L000651	    \right]\\
L000652	    &\qquad+ (1-\sqrt{1-\tau})^2 (n\hat{M}n) (\bar{n} M \bar{n})\\
L000653	    &= M \hat{M}
L000654	\end{split}
L000655	\end{align}
L000656	
L000657	
L000658	From Eq.~\eqref{eq:ana_class_bulk_deri} we can now derive straightforwardly
L000659	\begin{align} \label{eq:ana_class_bulk}
L000660	     [\hat{F},F] = [\hat{E},E] = 0, \qquad
L000661	     (1-\tau) \hat{F} E  = F\hat{E}, \qquad
L000662	     \hat{E}F + \tau \hat{F} E = E \hat{F}
L000663	\end{align}
L000664	
L000665	
L000666	The boundary conditions can be treated similarly and yield
L000667	\begin{align}
L000668	    \label{eq:ana_class_bc}
L000669	    \bra{W}(F+\alpha E)=\bra{W}\hat{F}, \quad
L000670	     \bra{W} E (1-\alpha) = \bra{W} \hat E, \quad
L000671	     (1-\beta)\hat F\ket{V} = F\ket{V},\quad
L000672	     (\hat E + \hat F \beta)\ket{V} = E\ket{V}.
L000673	 \end{align}
L000674	
L000675	
L000676	
L000677	
L000678	Eqs.~(\ref{eq:ana_class_bulk}) and~(\ref{eq:ana_class_bc}) can be solved by mapping the operator algebra to that of the classical PASEP (partially asymmetric simple exclusion process)~\cite{Blythe07}.
L000679	
L000680	To this end we make the ansatz $\hat{F} = F - \lambda\mathds{1}$, $\hat{E} = E + \lambda\mathds{1}$ such that the bulk commutator conditions are trivially satisfied.
L000681	
L000682	The remaining two bulk equations now coincide and similar for the boundary equations.
L000683	
L000684	Hence, we are left with the following conditions
L000685	
L000686	
L000687	 \begin{align}
L000688	     \lambda F + \lambda (1-\tau) E = - \tau FE, \qquad
L000689	     \bra{W} E = -\frac{\lambda}{\alpha} \bra{W}, \qquad
L000690	      -\frac{\beta}{\lambda(1-\beta)} F \ket{V} = \ket{V}
L000691	 \end{align}
L000692	
L000693	
L000694	 Introducing rescaled matrices and parameters as in
L000695	 $F' = F/\sqrt{1-\tau}$, $E' = \sqrt{1-\tau} E$, $\alpha' = \alpha/\tau$, $\beta' = \beta/\tau \cdot (1-\tau)/ (1-\beta)$
L000696	and fixing $\lambda = -\tau/\sqrt{1-\tau}$ maps our algebra to the PASEP algebra, viz.,
L000697	 \begin{align}
L000698	    F' +  E' =  F'E', \qquad
L000699	    \bra{W} E' = \frac{1}{\alpha'} \bra{W},\ \qquad
L000700	     F' \ket{V} = \frac{1}{\beta'} \ket{V} .
L000701	 \end{align}
L000702	
L000703	
L000704	We may now use the well-established solution to the PASEP algebra ~\cite{Blythe07}.
L000705	
L000706	To this end we introduce the infinite dimensional matrix ${\cal M} = \mathds{1} + \sum_{n=1}^\infty \ket{n}\bra{n+1} + \sqrt{(\alpha' + \beta' - 1)/\alpha' \beta'} \ket{0}\bra{1}$ and express the solution as
L000707	
L000708	
L000709	\begin{align}\label{discrete_DE}
L000710	F = (1-\tau)^{\frac{1}{2}} \left( {\cal M} + (\beta'^{-1} - 1) \ket{0}\bra{0}   \right), \quad
L000711	E = (1-\tau)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \left( {\cal M}^T + (\alpha'^{-1} -1) \ket{0}\bra{0}  \right), \quad
L000712	\ket{V} = \ket{W} = \ket{0}.
L000713	\end{align}
L000714	
L000715	
L000716	
L000717	\subsection{Phase diagram}
L000718	The analytical solution for the classical NESS that we presented in the previous section is an infinite bond dimension matrix product state.
L000719	
L000720	We can truncate the bond dimension and evaluate the local occupations in order to determine the phase diagram of the TASEP.
L000721	
L000722	Here, as in the main test, we focus on the bulk hopping probability $\tau = 0.75$.
L000723	
L000724	\begin{figure*}[t!]
