L000779	\section{The continuous-time limit}
L000780	
L000781	In this section we illustrate that the discrete-time dynamics we consider can also encompass the continuous-time case in the correct scaling limit.
L000782	
L000783	Therefore, all probabilities of the stochastic processes need to scale with a time increment $dt$ which allows us to introduce the corresponding rate of the process.
L000784	
L000785	In order to obtain the continuous-time limit of our dynamical map $\mathcal{K}$ we need to identify $\alpha = \gamma_L dt$, $\tau = \gamma dt$ and $\beta = \gamma_R dt$.
L000786	
L000787	We then expand the Kraus map in powers of $dt$ up to linear order.
L000788	
L000789	For example, for the left boundary this prescription yields
L000790	$
L000791	K_{\circ}^{(L)} \simeq \mathds{1}  - \frac{1}{2}\gamma_L dt (\mathds{1}-\ket{\bullet}\bra{\bullet}) +  {\cal O} (dt^2)$ and $
L000792	K_{\bullet}^{(L)} \simeq i\sqrt{\gamma_L dt}\ket{\bullet}\bra{\circ} + {\cal O} (dt)$.
L000793	
L000794	Note that in order to obtain all linear terms we need to expand $K_{\circ}^{(L)}$ to linear order due to the mixed terms that the Kraus map generates but $K_{\bullet}^{(L)}$ only to $O(\sqrt{dt})$ as there are no cross terms.
L000795	
L000796	We then obtain the usual Lindblad master equation for the pump at the left boundary, viz.,
L000797	
L000798	
L000799	\begin{align}
L000800	    \rho(t+dt) =  \rho(t) + \gamma_L \left( \sigma_0^+\rho(t) \sigma_0^- -\frac{1}{2} \{\sigma_0^- \sigma_0^+,\rho(t)\}\right) dt
L000801	\end{align}
L000802	
L000803	
L000804	with the ladder operators $\sigma^+ = \ket{\bullet}\bra{\circ}$ and $\sigma^- = \ket{\circ}\bra{\bullet}$.
L000805	
L000806	The right boundary, as well as the bulk, can be treated in a similar fashion and we eventually find the full Lindbladian dynamics
L000807	\begin{align}
L000808	\begin{split}
L000809	    \frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t} = \mathcal{D}_{\rm TASEP}(\rho) \equiv \gamma_L \left( \sigma_0^+\rho(t) \sigma_0^- -\frac{1}{2}\{ \sigma_0^- \sigma_0^+,\rho(t)\} \right)
L000810	    + \gamma_R \left( \sigma_{N-1}^-\rho(t) \sigma_{N-1}^+ -\frac{1}{2} \{\sigma_{N-1}^+ \sigma_{N-1}^-,\rho(t)\}\right) \\
L000811	    +\sum_{j=1}^{N-1} \gamma \left( \sigma_{i-1}^-\sigma_{i}^+\rho(t)\sigma_{i-1}^+ \sigma_i^- -\frac{1}{2}\{ n_{i-1} (1-n_i),\rho(t)\} \right) \ .
L000812	\end{split}
L000813	\end{align}
L000814	Here, $n = \sigma^+ \sigma^-$ is the number operator.
L000815	
L000816	\section{PPT criterion for the NESS for system size $N=30$}
L000817	
L000818	While it is challenging to evaluate entanglement witnesses such as the negativity for larger systems, it has been recently shown that moment ratios of the partial transpose can act as indicator for PPT violations.
L000819	
L000820	More precisely Ref.~\cite{Elben20} has shown that $\rho$ is PPT implies that $1\geq p_2^2/p_3$ with $p_n = \operatorname{tr}((\rho^\Gamma)^n)$.
L000821	
L000822	In this section we evaluate this moment ratio for the NESS with which we obtained the phase diagram in Fig.~\ref{fig:PDq}(a) for $N=30$.
L000823	
L000824	The result is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:p2^2/p3}.
L000825	
L000826	We see that throughout the whole parameter range we consistently find $p_2^2/p_3 \leq 1$.
L000827	
L000828	Hence, this criterion fails at detecting any PPT violations in the NESS and indicates that the NESS is at least valid with the PPT criterion~\cite{Horodecki09} which is consistent with our observation that bipartite entanglement seems to be absent in the NESS.
L000829	
L000830	
L000831	\begin{figure}[b]
L000832	\centering
L000833	    \includegraphics[width= 0.36\textwidth]{FigS3.png}
L000834	    \caption{{\bf $p_2^2/p_3$ Phase Diagram for $N=30$}: The ratio  $p_2^2/p_3\le1$ everywhere implies no bipartite entanglement survives in the NESS. The ratio is exactly one at the edges as NESS is a pure state. }
L000835	    \label{fig:p2^2/p3}
L000836	\end{figure}
L000837	
L000838	
L000839	
